---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PlayImitatorGame from '../../../nn-chess/ui/PlayImitatorGame.tsx';
---
<BaseLayout title="nn-chess — play the imitator" description="Play directly against the player-imitator network" useMath={false} pwa="nn-chess">
  <h1>nn-chess — play the imitator</h1>
  <p>
    <a href="/nn-chess">← back to play the opponent</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Play directly against the <em>player-imitator</em> network — the
    behaviour-clone of your own moves that the main page trains in the
    background. Two reasons this exists:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Interpretability.</strong> Does the imitator actually play like
      you? Playing against it is the most direct way to find out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>More data per session.</strong> Every move you play here is
      recorded into the imitator's replay buffer at game end, exactly like the
      main page, so you can pile on training examples without having to slog
      through a full game against the (early, terrible) opponent network.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Move selection is from the imitator's raw policy head — no MCTS, no value
    search. That keeps the imitator a pure behaviour-cloning model of you,
    consistent with the
    <a href="https://github.com/lucaswiman/lucaswiman.github.io/blob/main/nn-chess/README.md">README's</a>
    rule against adding chess heuristics.
  </p>

  <div style="max-width: 620px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <PlayImitatorGame client:only="react" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
